  "body": "See [web compat issue 32547](https://github.com/webcompat/web-bugs/issues/32547) and [crbug.com/972180](https://crbug.com/972180) for more details and history.\r\n\r\nWhen encoding to JPEG, FireFox treats a quality of 90 or above as a request to disable subsampling. Chromium, meanwhile, only disables subsampling at quality 100 (CanvasAsyncBlobCreator and ImageDataBuffer).\r\n\r\nBy turning off chroma subsampling at a lower quality, FireFox is able to create much smaller Jpegs that still have relatively good looking text (i.e. not blurry).\r\n\r\nFor compatibility, the two browsers should have the same behavior, but it's not obvious (at least to me) what the right behavior is. According to the [Chromium bug](https://crbug.com/972180), PhotoShop uses a quality of 50 or higher to turn off chroma subsampling. What do other browsers do? It would be ideal if there is a spec somewhere for how to behave.\r\n\r\nA tangential idea is to add a new API that allows specifying whether to use chroma subsampling.",
